  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Spotty light showers/drizzle tonight but much \nof the time it will be dry. More humid Sun with a few showers & \nt-storms possible especially near & west of the CT River Valley \nwhere an isolated strong-severe t-storm or two is possible.\n\nWeak offshore low pressure will continue to result in \nmoist/light NE low level flow into tonight. The result will be \nrather cloudy skies tonight with just some spotty light \nshowers/drizzle at times...but this activity will be quite light\nwith much of the time featuring dry weather. Some shortwave \nenergy may allow for a few elevated heavier showers and perhaps \na couple t-storms to develop near the southeast New England \ncoast toward daybreak...mainly near the Cape and Islands. Some \nof the earlier model guidance has this activity further \nwest...but latest indicating that the main risk for this will be\ntowards the Cape and Islands closer to the shortwave energy.\n\nOvernight low temps will only drop into the lower to middle 60s\ngiven clouds and an abundance of low level moisture. Areas of \nfog will be possible too...especially near the coast and in the \nWorcester Hills.\n\nAgain...we will need to watch for a few showers and isolated t-\nstorms near the Cape and Islands early Sun morning. \nOtherwise...the main concern for a few showers and thunderstorms\nwill be Sun afternoon/early evening given diurnal instability \nand shortwave energy to our west. The bulk of the height \nfalls/forcing will be to our west. Therefore...greatest risk for\na few showers and thunderstorms will be near and west of the CT\nRiver Valley Sun afternoon/early evening. Instability will be \nlimited by a lot of cloud cover...but effective shear of 40+ \nknots will result in the low risk for an isolated strong to \nsevere severe thunderstorm or two. Main severe weather risk \nwould be a very localized damaging wind gusts and brief \ntorrential rainfall. Greatest risk for this would near and west \nof the CT River Valley. That being said...much of the day will \nfeature dry weather...but with a bit more humidity compared to \nthis afternoon. Highs will be in the 70s to near 80.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier with a return to seasonable temperatures \nnext week. Rain chances return Thursday/Friday.\n\nA post-frontal air mass will usher in drier conditions and \nwarmer temperatures with westerly winds Monday. As skies \ngradually clear from the dreary weekend, dewpoints will begin to\ndrop slightly... into the upper 50s as opposed to the low to \nmid 60s we've been seeing. High pressure begins to slowly build \nTuesday into Wednesday, prolonging the clear skies, drier \nweather, and warmer temperatures through the middle of the week.\nChances for rain showers return Thursday and Friday as a low \npressure system develops over the Great Lakes and meanders east \nthrough southern Ontario. Some uncertainty regarding the onset \nof the showers as there's little consistency in the global \nensembles on the actual placement of the low as it tracks \nthrough Canada.",
